## 🔩 Key Engineering Concepts

### 1. servo-actuated Actuation Me hanics
Fingers are moved using cable/servo routing connected to servo motors.

### 2. Open-Loop Control
The system currently operates without feedback sensors.

### 3. PWM-Based Control
Servo motors are controlled using PWM signals generated by firmware.

### 4. Layered Architecture
The system is divided into:

- Input Layer (user intent)
- Control Layer (software logic)
- Firmware Layer (real-time execution)
- Hardware Layer (physical motion)

---

## Calibration System

The system requires calibration for:

- Servo neutral positions
- Finger zero alignment
- servo tension balancing
- Movement range limits

Without calibration, motion becomes inconsistent or inaccurate.

---

##  Finger Model (Summary)

Each finger is mapped to one or more servos:

- Thumb → multi-DOF (most complex)
- Index → primary grasp control
- Middle → stability support
- Ring → grip reinforcement
- Pinky → balance and fine control

Each joint is driven by a servo-to-servo mapping system.

---

##  Communication

- Protocol: Serial (USB/UART)
- Data format: structured command packets
- Optional future extension: ROS integration

---

##  Current Limitations

- No sensor feedback (open-loop system)
- No force sensing
- No adaptive control
- Calibration must be manual

---

##  Future Improvements

- Closed-loop control system
- Force/position sensors
- ROS2 integration
- Auto-calibration routines
- Motion planning algorithms

